I don't understand why you say "even if". Indeed, if I'd have the wand
of cancellation in a sack I risk to put the sack into the bad of holding
unintentionally. But if I carry it in my open inventory I see that wand
and can act accordingly. I fostered a couple of standard procedures to
minimise risk of typos. First, the wand of cancellation and the bag of
holding both get bright colours (NAO feature) so that I immediately spot
them in my inventory. Then I put those items at standard letters (b: bag
[of holding], c: wand of cancellation). And finally, most important, in
most cases I select objects by their type, not individually; putting all
scrolls of potions in your container I use the selectors ! and ? for it
(not, say, e g h o x A N M, their individual letters), so that a mistyped
c instead of x will not happen for other object classes. WRT wands I
usually select individually because of the necessity to have defensive
wands in the open inventory; c coloured and at a prominent place at the
top of the list of wands. That works nicely.

I always wondered why some people put their wands of cancellation hidden
in a sack, and name the sack something like "dangerous" (in all caps),
instead of just naming (and/or colouring) the dangerous object itself.

